Roast.pet Frontier Stack =E2=80=94 2026-09-20 07:09 Asia/Phnom_Penh =E2=80=
=94 1 useful find

ACCEPTED ITEM: NVIDIA AI for Media R22 / Video Super Resolution update =E2=
=80=94 TEST

Source: https://developer.nvidia.com/topics/ai/generative-ai/ai-for-media
Release/update: NVIDIA AI for Media R22 announced September 11, 2026;
NVIDIA's September IBC materials detail the expanded VSR capability.

What changed: NVIDIA Video Super Resolution now has new streaming
modes for choosing real-time performance vs higher image quality,
adjustable enhancement controls, 10-bit video support, and improved
overall performance/quality. NVIDIA exposes VSR through the Video
Effects SDK and as a NIM microservice; the current developer page also
says VSR can upscale 16:9 content from 480p up to 8K with controls for
sharpness, blur, denoising and hallucination limits, and is available
as a Python wheel.

Roast.pet capability: this creates a deterministic post-render
enhancement stage for cheap/fast 480p or 768p talking-pet generations.
Instead of paying the video model itself for 1080p/2K on every
attempt, Roast.pet can potentially generate/rank at lower resolution
and upscale only the accepted winner while reducing compression/noise
artifacts.

Inputs/outputs: rendered video -> enhanced/upscaled video.
API/install: NVIDIA Video Effects SDK / Python wheel / NIM
microservice on supported NVIDIA hardware.
Pricing/cost: no simple per-clip public tariff verified. Local
deployment consumes NVIDIA GPU compute; NIM/production use is under
NVIDIA AI Enterprise terms. NVIDIA advertises an API catalog
playground and 90-day evaluation path.
Commercial license: governed by NVIDIA Software License Agreement /
Product-Specific Terms; associated models use NVIDIA Community/Open
Model licenses depending on component. Verify exact production terms
before shipping.
Privacy/self-hosting: strong. Can run locally/on-prem rather than
uploading customer-derived videos to another creative SaaS.
Quality/latency constraints: RTX/NVIDIA hardware required; upscaling
cannot recover pet identity already destroyed by the generator and can
itself hallucinate fine detail, so fur/marking regression tests are
mandatory.
Exact Roast.pet use case: Pet video renderer outputs cheap 480p/768p
draft -> AV/identity QC -> accepted winner -> VSR ->
caption/composite/export. This is especially interesting because H3
Max currently has substantial price steps between output resolutions.
Smallest prototype: take 30 accepted 480p/768p pet videos plus 10
intentionally bad ones. Run VSR at 2x with conservative hallucination
settings. Blind-score fur texture, eye/whisker detail, marking
fidelity, mouth edges, compression artifacts and perceived quality
against native higher-resolution generations. Record GPU seconds and
compare total usable-output cost.
Decision: TEST. Do not adopt until pet identity/fur fidelity beats
native high-res economically.

TOP 5 STACK CHANGES TO TEST NOW
1. Low-res video generation -> winner selection -> NVIDIA VSR upscale
versus native high-res generation.
2. Qwen3.8-Omni-Flash as unified audiovisual ProductionQC.
3. H3 Max vs LongCat vs Wan/HD-V1 on the fixed hard-pet corpus.
4. Local PetPrep -> fur/edge QC -> hosted fallback.
5. N-way TTS -> STT integrity -> ComedyRanker -> animate winner ->
deterministic AV gates -> multimodal QC.

CHEAPEST meaningful capability improvement
Still local PetPrep / deterministic QC, effectively zero marginal API
cost. The new economic experiment is whether low-resolution generation
plus local VSR beats paying frontier video APIs for high-resolution
output.

BIGGEST CAPABILITY SHOCK THIS HOUR
Not a new generator: production-grade enhancement is becoming modular
enough that generation resolution and final delivery resolution can be
decoupled. That can materially change Roast.pet unit economics because
failed/bad candidates need never be rendered at premium resolution.

CONCRETE STACK SIMPLIFICATION
Make resolution enhancement a post-QC concern:
PetAsset -> Voice/Comedy selection -> VideoRenderer(cheap resolution)
-> ProductionQC -> Enhancer(VSR) -> Captions/QR/Export.
Never upscale rejected candidates.

No other sufficiently fresh, verified Roast.pet-specific release
cleared the inclusion threshold this run; mature unchanged tools and
marketing rewrites were excluded.
